ature, and a reasonable person concludes the two are"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"connected. They are not."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Remote credentials live in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"rclone.conf"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"obscured"}]},{"type":"text","text":", which is obfuscation and not"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"encryption — "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"rclone reveal <value>"},{"type":"text","text":" returns the original in one step. So on a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"machine with an unencrypted config, every remote password, S3 key and OAuth token"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"is readable by anything that can read the file: any process running as that user,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"any backup, any folder-syncing tool that happens to include "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"~\/.config"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Encrypting the config is what makes those credentials actually protected, and the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"keychain then holds the one password that opens it. Until then, the keychain"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"feature guards something the user does not have."}]}]}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/grrclonecore\/configencryption"]}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/docs.GrrCloneCore\/documentation\/GrrCloneCore\/ConfigEncryption","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"references":{"doc://docs.GrrCloneCore/documentation/GrrCloneCore/ConfigEncryption/Failure":{"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Failure","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/grrclonecore\/configencryption\/failure","type":"topic","role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","title":"ConfigEncryption.Failure","abstract":[],"identifier